A view controller (UIViewController subclass) manages one screen/scene: it owns the view hierarchy, handles user interaction, and coordinates data flow between the model and the views.

func factorial(_ n: Int) -> Int {
    var result = 1
    if n > 1 { for i in 2...n { result *= i } }
    return result
}
print(factorial(5))   // 120

View hierarchy: views (UIView instances) are nested in a tree — a root view contains subviews, which may contain their own subviews. The hierarchy defines rendering order, layout and event routing; Auto Layout constraints keep it adaptive across screen sizes and orientations.

func sum(_ arr: [Int]) -> Int {
    var total = 0
    for value in arr { total += value }
    return total
}
print(sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))   // 15

